  "intro": "The U.S. exported **$12.61B** of copper and copper products in 2025, up 3.2% from 2024 and almost double 2020's $6.45B. Nearly half (**48%**, $6.01B) was copper scrap (7404): bare bright, No. 1 and No. 2 copper and brass scrap. Next came refined copper (7403, $1.96B, mostly cathode) and copper wire, mostly wire rod (7408, $1.44B). Mexico and Canada took **51%**. The big shift was China: it bought $781M, down 73% from $2.94B, as U.S. scrap went to Japan, Korea, Thailand, India and Belgium instead. Exports are up **30.3%** in 2026 so far: $10.70B from January to August.\n\nTariffs bite in two places. **China** charges **35%** on U.S. copper scrap and cathode unless the Chinese importer holds an exclusion: a 0% normal rate, plus its 25% counter-tariff from 2018–2019, plus the 10% extra duty on all U.S. goods. **Canada** has charged a **50% surtax** on U.S. refined copper wire since September 8, 2026. Elsewhere, scrap enters duty-free in every large market: Mexico, Canada, the EU, Japan, Korea, Thailand and, since February 2025, India; cathode does too everywhere except India (5%). The EU has charged 0% on every U.S. copper product since July 1, 2026. In these markets the buyer's scrap-import rules decide access, not the tariff: China's recycled-copper standards, India's radiation certificates, the EU's waste-shipment documents.",
